## Expected Benchmark Outcomes

| Benchmark | Current (5.1) | Expected (5.2) | Rationale |
|-----------|--------------|----------------|-----------|
| `select_full_scan_1k` | 1,888 TPS | **~4,000-6,000 TPS** | Sort eliminated. Copies remain but pre-allocated. Won't fully recover to Phase 4's 49K because map iteration replaces slice indexing. |
| `select_full_scan_10k` | 133 TPS | **~400-600 TPS** | Same rationale. The copy cost scales linearly. |
| `select_scan_scaling_10000` | 121 TPS | **~400-600 TPS** | Same. |
| `select_with_predicate` | 105 TPS | **~300-500 TPS** | Phase 3 selective copy — only ~50% of rows copied. |
| `select_with_projection` | 83 TPS | **~200-400 TPS** | Projection happens after scan; Phase 3 selective copy helps. |
| `select_range_scan_100` | 112 TPS | **~300-500 TPS** | Range queries still go through the scan executor. |
| `join_nested_loop_100_x_100` | 4,032 TPS | **~8,000-15,000 TPS** | `buildJoinIndex` zero-copy eliminates 200 copies per join side. |
| `join_nested_loop_1k_x_1k` | 385 TPS | **~800-1,500 TPS** | Same — 2,000 fewer copies. |
| `insert_with_fk` | 128,867 TPS | **~130,000+ TPS** | Minor improvement — FK validation slow path uses iterator. |
| `update_by_pk` | 81 TPS | **~120-200 TPS** | Executor pre-scan loops use iterator (no copies). |
| `delete_by_pk` | 89 TPS | **~130-200 TPS** | Same. |
| `select_pk_lookup` | 153,717 TPS | **~153,000 TPS** | Unchanged — already uses B+Tree `Search()`. |
| `mixed_read_write_80_20` | 133,724 TPS | **~135,000+ TPS** | Dominated by PK lookups. Minor scan improvement. |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **We will NOT fully recover to Phase 4 scan numbers.** Phase 4 used direct slice indexing (`table.Rows[i]`) with perfect cache locality. Phase 5.1's `map[int64]data.Row` has inherent hash-table overhead and pointer chasing. The tradeoff is intentional: O(log N) PK lookups and O(1) mutations are worth the scan cost. A further recovery path would be a sorted-array secondary structure rebuilt lazily on snapshot, but that's Phase 6+ territory.

---

## Additional Finding: `insert_with_wal` at 145 TPS

| Benchmark | Phase 3 | Phase 5.1 | Notes |
|-----------|---------|-----------|-------|
| `insert_with_wal` | 260K TPS (100% errors) | 145 TPS (0 errors) | **Not a scan regression** |
| `insert_no_wal` | 145K TPS | 122K TPS | Healthy |

The WAL benchmark now correctly writes to disk (0 errors — fixed in 5.1), but at 145 TPS (~7ms per insert), this strongly suggests **fsync per operation**. Each insert triggers a WAL append + fsync, which is correct for durability but extremely slow for benchmarks.

> [!NOTE]
> This is **not a 5.2 issue**. It's WAL I/O behavior that was previously masked by errors. Options for future phases:
> - Group commit (batch multiple operations per fsync) — already partly implemented in Phase 3
> - The benchmark may be running each insert as an implicit single-op transaction, bypassing the group commit buffer
> - Investigate whether the benchmark harness wraps inserts in transactions or runs them standalone

---

## Open Questions

None that block implementation. The design decisions are straightforward extensions of the Phase 5.1 architecture.

If the scan recovery numbers are insufficient after Phase 3, the next lever is:
- **Lazy snapshot array**: Rebuild a sorted `[]data.Row` slice from `RowsByRID` after mutations quiesce, and serve scans from it until the next mutation invalidates it. This is essentially a read cache. Add when benchmarks demand it.

---

